# What must never reach a build context.
#
# **`__pycache__` is the one that actually bit.** Every image is built with `context: ..`, so a
# `COPY libs /app/libs` copied the host's compiled bytecode in alongside the source. Python loads a
# `.pyc` whenever it considers it current, so a container could execute stale bytecode while the
# `.py` beside it was correct — which is precisely what happened four times on 2026-08-01: a build
# reported success, the source in the image was right, and the running service still had a column
# that had been dropped. `--no-cache` "fixed" it only because it discarded the bytecode too.
#
# The rest is size. The context was the whole repository: a virtualenv, the git history, and
# node_modules, re-sent on every build of every service.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# The host's virtualenv. Linux-or-Windows, wrong Python, and irrelevant: each image installs from
# its own requirements.txt.
.venv/
venv/

.git/
.github/

# Installed and built on the host. The api image runs `npm ci` and `npm run build` itself, and
# copying either of these over the top would replace what it just built with whatever the host had.
web/node_modules/
web/dist/
node_modules/

# Test and tooling artefacts.
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
htmlcov/
.coverage

# Local operator state that is not part of any image.
*.log
logs/
scratch/
